  {
    "event": {
      "address": "20 Maresfield Gardens, London, NW3 5SX",
      "all_day": false,
      "category": "talk",
      "dedupe_key": "in-person-symposium-book-launch-of-psychoanalysis-otherwise-with-raluca-soreanu-ana-minozzo|2026-11-16|freud",
      "description": "This ticket is for IN-HOUSE access to this event. Please click here if you would like to purchase an online ticket. ________________________________________________________________________ The Freud Museum is delighted to host the launch of ‘Psychoanalysis Otherwise’, a collection edited by the FREEPSY Collective. This evening symposium is dedicated to the publication (6:00pm – 7:45pm), with the presence of editors Raluca Soreanu and Ana Minozzo, authors and guests. It is followed by launch of an exhibition by Ana Čvorović, curated by Sacha Craddock (8pm – 9pm), and a wine reception (9pm – 10pm). The symposium is available both in person and online. The first part of the evening will be dedicated to the launch of Psychoanalysis Otherwise, a collection of papers by the Free Clinics Working Group (FCWG) at FREEPSY. The Free Clinics Working Group is a collective space of exchange, learning, thinking and writing promoted by FREEPSY. Over the past four years, FREEPSY hosted guests from free clinics and scholars from various territories aiming at stretching our collective repertoire of what free psychoanalytic clinics are, can be and how they operate across different contexts. Since 2022, the FCWG has been fostering the establishment of an intimate network of researchers & clinicians of psychosocial orientation that can discuss texts, think together and produce alternative types of scholarly work collectively such as zines, online dictionaries, artwork and essays. Psychoanalysis Otherwise is the fruit of these collective explorations. On the evening, psychoanalysts Raluca Soreanu and Ana Minozzo will be in conversation with authors and guests, including Susana Caló and Anderson Santos. The date also marks the first day of the three-week Free Clinics Festival, organised by the FREEPSY Collective. Held between November 16 and December 6 in various locations across London, the Free Clinics Festival includes a series workshops, book launches, conferences, artist talks, exhibitions and more. Please check the FREEPSY website for more information on how to register for the events: https://freepsyproject.com/fcf/. The second part of the evening will be dedicated to the opening of an exhibition by Ana Čvorović, artist-researcher at FREEPSY, curated by Sacha Craddock. The exhibition will be displayed on site, at the Freud Museum, over the duration of the Free Clinics Festival. The two launches will be followed by a reception. Please click here if you would like to purchase an online ticket. __________________________________________________________________ Speakers: Susana Caló is a researcher and writer. Her work focuses on reconstructing potential histories of radical psy practices, considering their relationship to wider social, political and urban struggles with the aim to develop a critical theory of psychological practices and contemporary psychopolitics. Her most recent publication CERFI: Analysis Everywhere (Minor Compositions) is the result of ten years of collective research into the history and practices of French institutional analysis and psychotherapy, examining their conceptual and socio-political dimensions. She was a co-investigator in The Pragmatic Genealogy of Concepts (BA, King’s College London), examining the political life of concepts in post-war French thought. She is a founding member of Chaosmosemedia, a rhizomatic project that has organised several international events focusing on the work of the psychoanalyst and activist Félix Guattari. She lectures at the Open University and collaborates with the FREEPSY collective, acting as a consultant and network organiser contributing to the development of a Free Clinics Network. Sacha Craddock (b. 1955) is an art critic, writer & curator based in London. She is co-founder of ArtSchool Palestine, member of Abbey Council, and co-founder of the Contemporary Art Award at the British School at Rome, Trustee of the Shelagh Cluett Trust, and President of AICA-UK. She was Chair of the Board of New Contemporaries and selection process from 1996 until 2021. Selected critical writing includes essays on Mark Boulos, Angus Fairhurst, Jose Dávila, Chantal Joffe, Wolfgang Tillmans, Rosa Lee, and Adam Henein, among others. She was the co-founder of Bloomberg Space and its curator from 2002-2011. Her curatorial contribution includes the Turner Prize, Hull 2017, Strike Site at Backlit Gallery, Nottingham 2018, Here, Now at Misk Art Institute 2021, Glossary for Curated by, Vienna 2023, and Thought to Image for Albion Jeune 2024. Latest curatorial projects include PLATAFORMA as International Guest Curator, Guadalajara 2025-2026, and David Garner: I have nothing to say only to show, Penarth 2025. Ana Čvorović (b. Sarajevo 1981) is a contemporary artist based in London. Her installations are an exploration of the psychodynamics of place, specifically in the context of war, migration, and socio-economic impoverishment. Her solo exhibitions include Archipelago at Ballon Rouge, …",
      "ends_at": "2026-11-16T21:00:00Z",
