  {
    "event": {
      "address": "20 Maresfield Gardens, London, NW3 5SX",
      "all_day": false,
      "category": "talk",
      "dedupe_key": "freuds-afternoon-session-midlife|2026-11-26|freud",
      "description": "“Throughout my therapeutic career most of my clients have been between the age of 35 and 55 and arrive in the state of anxiety, overwhelmed by some intractable problem. We have come to call this the midlife crisis.” – Andrew Jamieson Andrew Jamieson identifies that only two species of mammal have an extensive, post-reproductive life: killer whales – whose elders can sniff out food for their pods – and Homo sapiens. While the rationale behind the whale’s longevity seems clear, what is the point of ours? This question intrigued Carl Jung who observed that if a culture is to thrive, it must find a balance between the energy of the young and the experience of the old. But to obtain this equilibrium, we need to pass through the dreaded middle years. Examining the Jungian concept of the midlife crisis, and the lives of prominent figures who endured it (including Abraham Lincoln and Marie Curie), psychoanalyst Andrew Jamieson shows in his book how there is an evolutionary purpose behind this rite of passage which ­­– once traversed – holds the key to our prosperity. The Freud Museum welcomes Andrew Jamieson for this Afternoon Session, in which Andrew will deliver a short presentation on his book, followed by a conversation with Dr Martin Gledhill. _______________________________________________________________________ Speaker: Andrew Jamieson is a psychotherapist based in Bath whose interests include psychotherapy’s interconnection with philosophy, music and literature. He is the author of Midlife: Humanity’s Secret Weapon and On the Couch: 20 extraordinary personalities from Picasso to Putin (both published by Notting Hill Editions). Parallel to his psychotherapeutic career, Jamieson has promoted orchestral concerts throughout the UK for over forty years. Dr Martin Gledhill was a senior lecturer in the Bath University Architectural Department. He has always been fascinating by psychology and completed a PhD on the interface between architecture and psychology. Recently he has published a well received book on Jung’s Bollingen Tower and is currently working on a second book to be published by Routledge exploring further the overlap between psychology and architecture. He is the Chairman of the Trustees at the Bath Centre for Psychotherapy and Counselling. After a serendipitous first meeting following a lecture, he has known Andrew Jamieson for a number of years. During enjoyably long walks in the Wiltshire and Somerset countryside they have discussed the many aspects of psychoanalysis and how it might aid our interpretation of an increasingly troubled world as much as ourselves. _______________________________________________________________________ Complement your visit to the Freud Museum with one of our tours or talks led by staff, volunteers and guest speakers. Afternoon Sessions are free with your admission ticket, you can book your admission ticket now! Please note that we have limited capacity so advanced booking is recommended to avoid missing out. The event will be held on the first floor of the Museum during regular opening hours. Unfortunately, the Freud Museum does not currently have step-free access. Advance booking is highly recommended, as capacity is limited. Concessions/Members/Patrons: Please show proof of eligibility/membership card on arrival.",
      "ends_at": "2026-11-26T16:00:00Z",
      "image_url": "https://www.freud.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Midlife-framed.jpg",
      "lat": null,
      "lng": null,
      "price": {
        "currency": "GBP",
        "is_free": false,
        "max": "14.90",
        "min": "12.90"
      },
      "starts_at": "2026-11-26T15:00:00Z",
      "tags": [
        "events",
        "talks"
      ],
      "title": "Freud’s Afternoon Session – Midlife",
      "url": "https://www.freud.org.uk/event/freuds-afternoon-session-midlife/",
      "venue_name": "Freud Museum London"
    },
    "id": "/event/freuds-afternoon-session-midlife/"
  },
  {
    "event": {
      "address": "20 Maresfield Gardens, London, NW3 5SX",
      "all_day": false,
      "category": "talk",
      "dedupe_key": "making-clinic-spatial-material-investigations-artist-talk-with-ana-čvorović|2026-11-27|freud",
      "description": "The Freud Museum is delighted to host an artist talk by Ana Čvorović, whose work is displayed at the Museum, in an exhibition curated by Sacha Craddock (November 16-December 6 2026 ). The talk will be followed by a wine reception ( 9 pm – 10 pm). In this artist talk, Ana Čvorović discusses the work she developed through her research with FREEPSY – Free Clinics and Psychoanalysis for the People, bringing together earlier iterations of the work made during the project with the new sculpture and sound installation exhibited at the Freud Museum. Taking the works as a starting point, Čvorović reflects on how sculpture, sound, objects and spatial practice have informed her thinking about the psychoanalytic clinic as a relational, social and political space. The talk considers questions such as: what enters the clinical space? What remains outside it? And how might the material, spatial and sonic conditions of the clinic shape psychic life, relationships, supports, boundaries and exclusions? The talk will be followed by a Q&A and a wine and canapés reception. This event is part of the three-week Free Clinics Festival, organised by the FREEPSY Collective. Held between November 16 and December 6 in various locations across London, the Free Clinics Festival includes a series workshops, book launches, conferences, artist talks, exhibitions and more. Please check the FREEPSY website for more information on how to register for the events: https://freepsyproject.com/fcf/. The FREEPSY Collective ( Free Clinics and a Psychoanalysis for the People: Progressive Histories, Collective Practices, Implications for Our Times ) is an interdisciplinary research project of nine researchers based at the University of Essex, studying the practices and legacies of free clinics around the world (UKRI Frontier Research Grant, PI Raluca Soreanu). __________________________________________________________________ Speakers: Ana Čvorović (b. Sarajevo 1981) is a contemporary artist based in London. Her installations are an exploration of the psychodynamics of place, specifically in the context of war, migration, and socio-economic impoverishment. Her solo exhibitions include Archipelago at Ballon Rouge, Brussels (2021) and Borders Unfold at Pi Artworks, London (2019). Recent residencies include Arthouse Jersey for the commissioned project No Place Like Home (2023); FAP, Lebanon (2022) supported by the Foundation for Art and Psychoanalysis; and Sculpture Space, New York (2018) supported by Arts Council England. She is an Artist Researcher on the UKRI-funded project FREEPSY: Free Clinics and a Psychoanalysis for the People at the University of Essex. _______________________________________________________________________ Solidarity Ticket – £26.94 Standard Ticket – £16.40 A limited number of bursary tickets (£11.13) are available for those under financial hardship. Please email perry@freud.org.uk to apply for a bursary. Freud Museum Members and Patrons receive 20% off the standard ticket price on all events, courses, conferences and On Demand programming. The purpose of this event is to raise funds for the Freud Museum London, which receives no regular Government income. We are grateful to you for supporting our independent museum as generously as possible. The event will be held on the first floor of the Museum during regular opening hours. Unfortunately the Freud Museum does not have step-free access at this time. Advance booking is highly recommended as capacity is limited.",
      "ends_at": "2026-11-27T21:00:00Z",
      "image_url": "https://www.freud.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Screenshot-2026-09-23-155647.png",
      "lat": null,
      "lng": null,
